              }
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      "knows": {
        "name": "Knows",
        "label": { "en-US": "Knows" },
        "color": "edge-color-seq-2"
      }
    }
  },
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "information-researcher-notes",
      "type": "Information",
      "label": { "en-US": "Template notes (delete before fielding)" },
      "title": { "en-US": "Template notes" },
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "researcher-notes",
          "type": "text",
          "content": {
            "en-US": "## Discussion prototype: family reunions\n\n_Delete this screen before collecting any data._\n\nBased on the existing Network Canvas CEGRM template. Review language, family boundaries and support domains with local collaborators. This version starts with mother/father wording; Architect also offers egg/sperm wording or participant choice. Neither setting describes an entire population.\n\nConsent, recruitment, privacy and recording procedures are placeholders requiring local ethics approval. Conduct each interview privately even if recruitment happens at a reunion. Never display one participant's health history to the reunion or merge relatives' maps by default.\n\nThe closing condition display uses unknown inheritance and hides inferred risk. It presents reported family history, not genetic counselling or risk assessment. The optional gender question controls shapes; AFAB/AMAB/UAAB annotations are not implemented. Record participant observations and map limitations as qualitative findings.\n\nCEGRM: Kenen & Peters (2001), https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1016627426430. Methods and clinical interpretation should be reviewed with Lori Erby and Laura Koehly."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "information-intro",
      "type": "EgoForm",
      "label": { "en-US": "Welcome and consent" },
      "introductionPanel": {
        "title": { "en-US": "About this interview" },
        "text": {
          "en-US": "## Your family and the people who support you\n\nThis demonstration maps family relationships, what you know about a family health condition, and the people you turn to for information or support. Use nicknames if you prefer. You can leave questions unanswered, pause, or stop.\n\nFor an actual study, the researcher must first explain the approved consent and privacy procedures. This example consent question is a placeholder."
        }
      },
      "form": {
        "fields": [
          {
            "variable": "participant_consent",
            "prompt": {
              "en-US": "Do you consent to participate in this study?"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ego-form-background",
      "type": "EgoForm",
      "label": { "en-US": "About you" },
      "skipLogic": {
        "action": "SKIP",
        "filter": {
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d40",
              "type": "ego",
              "options": {
                "attribute": "participant_consent",
                "operator": "EXACTLY",
                "value": false
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        "destination": {
          "type": "finish"
        }
      },
      "introductionPanel": {
        "title": { "en-US": "A little about you" },
        "text": { "en-US": "A few short questions before we start on the map." }
      },
      "form": {
        "fields": [
          {
            "variable": "ego_age",
            "prompt": { "en-US": "How old are you?" }
          },
          {
            "variable": "ego_education",
            "prompt": {
              "en-US": "What is the highest level of education you have completed?"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "family-pedigree",
      "type": "FamilyPedigree",
      "label": { "en-US": "Family pedigree" },
      "interviewScript": "Build the pedigree with the participant rather than handing them the device. Published CEGRM sessions are co-constructed, and recent studies pre-populate the pedigree before the session and review it with the participant instead of drawing it from scratch \u2014 do that if your study design allows, since it substantially reduces burden.\n\nLiving status and birth year are collected while each person is added. The two questions after the pedigree is finalised are asked by tapping people on the finished tree. Take them slowly. Illness, testing and bereavement will often prompt a story; let it run, and capture it using your approved study procedure.\n\nDiscussion prototype: mother/father framing is a starting point for collaborator review, not an assumption about all Black families or reunion participants. Ask local partners about preferred wording. Gender identity drives the configurable person shapes separately from sex recorded at birth. Standard AFAB/AMAB/UAAB annotations are not yet displayed.",
      "nodeConfig": {
        "type": "person",
        "nodeLabelVariable": "name",
        "egoVariable": "is_ego",
        "relationshipVariable": "relationship_to_ego",
        "biologicalSexVariable": "biologicalSex",
        "form": [
          {
            "variable": "gender_identity",
            "prompt": {
              "en-US": "How does this person describe their gender, if you know?"
            },
            "hint": {
              "en-US": "Optional. Do not guess. You may leave this blank."
            }
          },
          {
            "variable": "living_status",
            "prompt": { "en-US": "Is this person living or deceased?" }
          },
          {
            "variable": "birth_year",
            "prompt": { "en-US": "What year was this person born?" },
            "hint": { "en-US": "Leave this blank if you do not know." }
          }
        ]
      },
      "edgeConfig": {
        "type": "family_relationship",
        "relationshipTypeVariable": "relationshipType",
        "isActiveVariable": "isActive",
        "isGestationalCarrierVariable": "isGestationalCarrier",
        "gameteRoleVariable": "gameteRole"
      },
      "framing": {
        "mode": "fixed",
        "value": "gendered"
      },
      "boundaries": {
        "requireGrandparents": "recommended",
        "requireChildrenContributors": "off"
      },
      "introScreen": {
        "items": [
          {
            "id": "pedigree-intro",
            "type": "text",
            "content": {
              "en-US": "A family tree records everyone in your family — including partners, step-parents, adoptive and social parents, donors, surrogates, and anyone else who helped you come into the world.\n\nIt also keeps track of which relationships are **biological**, because that is what lets us follow how a condition may pass down a family. You will be asked about both, so please include everyone who belongs in your family story."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "censusPrompt": { "en-US": "Let's map out your family. Who is in it?" },
      "nominationPrompts": [
        {
          "id": "nom-condition",
          "text": { "en-US": "Who in your family has had **this condition**?" },
          "variable": "has_condition"
        },
        {
          "id": "nom-testing",
          "text": { "en-US": "Who has had **genetic testing** for it?" },
          "variable": "had_testing"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "information-beyond-family",
      "type": "Information",
      "label": { "en-US": "Beyond the family" },
      "title": { "en-US": "The people beyond your family" },
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "beyond-text",
          "type": "text",
          "content": {
            "en-US": "You have mapped the people who belong in your family story. The next few screens add everyone else who matters — friends, colleagues, neighbours, people from groups you belong to, and the people who look after your health.\n\nThey will sit alongside your family on the same map."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ng-non-kin",
      "type": "NameGeneratorQuickAdd",
      "label": { "en-US": "People in your life" },
      "interviewScript": "The same person can belong in several of these questions. Add them once, then pick them from the panel of people already named rather than typing the name again.\n\nThe published instrument treats this step as widening the map to the participant's whole social universe, so do not rush it \u2014 friends and co-members of groups are where the spiritual and emotional domains usually turn out to live.",
      "subject": {
        "entity": "node",
        "type": "person"
      },
      "quickAdd": "name",
      "panels": [
        {
          "id": "already-mentioned",
          "title": { "en-US": "People you added earlier" },
          "dataSource": "existing",
          "filter": {
            "join": "AND",
            "rules": [
              {
                "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d46",
                "type": "node",
                "options": {
                  "type": "person",
                  "attribute": "is_ego",
                  "operator": "NOT",
                  "value": true
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d47",
                "type": "node",
                "options": {
                  "type": "person",
                  "attribute": "relationship_to_ego",
                  "operator": "NOT_EXISTS"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "prompts": [
        {
          "id": "p-friends",
          "text": { "en-US": "Who are the **friends** who matter most to you?" }
        },
        {
          "id": "p-work",
          "text": {
            "en-US": "Who are the people you know through **work or study**?"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "p-groups",
          "text": {
            "en-US": "Who do you know through **groups you belong to** — a faith community, a team, a club, or a community you are part of online?"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "p-care",
          "text": {
            "en-US": "Who is involved in looking after your **health**?"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bin-tie-type",
      "type": "CategoricalBin",
      "label": { "en-US": "How you know each person" },
      "subject": {
        "entity": "node",
        "type": "person"
      },
      "prompts": [
        {
          "id": "p-tie-type",
          "text": {
            "en-US": "Sort the people you have just named by **how you know them**."
          },
          "variable": "tie_type",
          "otherVariable": "tie_type_other",
          "otherVariablePrompt": {
            "en-US": "How would you describe how you know this person?"
          },
          "otherOptionLabel": { "en-US": "Something else" }
        }
      ],
      "filter": {
        "join": "AND",
        "rules": [
          {
            "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d40",
            "type": "node",
            "options": {
              "type": "person",
              "attribute": "is_ego",
              "operator": "NOT",
              "value": true
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d44",
            "type": "node",
            "options": {
              "type": "person",
              "attribute": "relationship_to_ego",
              "operator": "NOT_EXISTS"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bin-closeness",
      "type": "OrdinalBin",
      "label": { "en-US": "How close you feel" },
      "interviewScript": "Closeness is a Network Canvas addition rather than a CEGRM symbol. It locates the support exchanges within the participant's perceived relationship structure and makes it possible to distinguish emotionally close ties from ties that perform a particular support function.",
      "subject": {
        "entity": "node",
        "type": "person"
      },
      "prompts": [
        {
          "id": "p-closeness",
          "text": {
            "en-US": "Sort each person by **how close you feel** to them now."
          },
          "variable": "closeness",
          "color": "ord-color-seq-1"
        }
      ],
      "filter": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d48",
            "type": "node",
            "options": {
              "type": "person",
              "attribute": "is_ego",
              "operator": "NOT",
              "value": true
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bin-contact-frequency",
      "type": "OrdinalBin",
      "label": { "en-US": "How often you are in contact" },
      "interviewScript": "Contact frequency is a Network Canvas addition rather than a CEGRM symbol. Ask about contact by any channel during the past 12 months. People recorded as deceased are excluded because a current-frequency category would be inapplicable; people whose living status is unknown remain available.",
      "subject": {
        "entity": "node",
        "type": "person"
      },
      "prompts": [
        {
          "id": "p-contact-frequency",
          "text": {
            "en-US": "Thinking about the past 12 months, sort each person by **how often you have been in contact** — in person, by phone, or online."
          },
          "variable": "contact_frequency",
          "color": "ord-color-seq-2"
        }
      ],
      "filter": {
        "join": "AND",
        "rules": [
          {
            "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d49",
            "type": "node",
            "options": {
              "type": "person",
              "attribute": "is_ego",
              "operator": "NOT",
              "value": true
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "c0a8f2d1-4b6e-4a9c-8f3d-2e7b1a5c9d50",
            "type": "node",
            "options": {
              "type": "person",
              "attribute": "living_status",
              "operator": "EXCLUDES",
              "value": "deceased"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "socio-exchanges",
      "type": "Sociogram",
